[resource]
script = ExtResource("3_n52vc")
original_image = SubResource("Resource_bcsnl")
piece_name = "Old Man Nishimura Seated and Holding the Hochi Newspaper"
artist = ExtResource("1_htjxr")
actual_start_year = 1875
actual_end_year = 1875
date_type = 1
edits_description = "Only the newspaper was used."
final_image_tex = SubResource("Resource_uqt8i")
blurb = "Depicts an old man holding a newspaper frowning at a peddler. The text above relates the story:

[i]A man calling himself the messenger of the Tengu of Mount Nikkō arrived at the house of Nishimura, a man famous for his \"high nose\" (pride and confidence).

The peddler took out several statues of Daikoku (one of Japan's Seven Lucky Gods) and said \"My master was moved by your faith, and sent me to grant you boundless fortune, longevity, and perfect harmony.\"

The old man did not fall for the trap and responded: \"I am a Great Tengu among merchants! A Leaf-Tengu like you is a fellow who wouldn't even be seen at the very lowest seat of my followers! Leave! Withdraw while your footing is still clear, or I'll teach you a lesson!\"

Glaring fiercely upwards over his glasses, the light in his eyes made the imposter tremble in fear and flee.

So it is told, this took place around the autumn of Meiji 7 (1874).

Recorded by Kishū Gyojin.[/i]

The Tengu mentioned are yōkai famous for having long noses, and the \"high nose\" of the old man is a pun on this. The Leaf-Tengu is a lower-ranking subtype of this species."
metadata/_custom_type_script = "uid://6kpsqyb2pk7a"
